# UI Toolkit (Unity style guide)

From [Unity's C# style guide](https://unity.com/resources/c-sharp-style-guide-unity-6). C# scripts: `unity-csharp-naming` + `unity-csharp-formatting`. **UXML/USS class names use BEM**, not C# PascalCase.

## UI Toolkit vs uGUI (Canvas)

**Default for Grid Dungeon:** **UI Toolkit** (`UIDocument`, UXML/USS, `VisualElement`) for player-facing HUD, menus, full-screen overlays (e.g. `ScreenFadePresenter`), and Editor tools ([`unity-editor-ui-toolkit.mdc`](unity-editor-ui-toolkit.mdc) in design-docs).

**Do not add new uGUI** (`UnityEngine.UI` — `Canvas`, `Image`, `Button`, legacy `Text`) without **explicit user approval**.

### Before implementing uGUI — ask first

If a task would use any of the following, **stop and ask the user** before coding. Propose a UITK approach and wait for confirmation:

- `Canvas`, `CanvasScaler`, `GraphicRaycaster`, `ScreenSpaceOverlay`
- `Image`, `RawImage`, `Mask`, uGUI `Text` / `Button`
- A second overlay stack that competes with existing `UIDocument` + `GamePanelSettings`

**UITK alternatives to suggest:** full-screen `VisualElement` + `style.backgroundColor` / opacity; shared `Assets/UI/Settings/GamePanelSettings.asset`; `UIDocument.sortingOrder` above HUD (e.g. screen fade at **10000**).

### When uGUI may be acceptable (still confirm with user)

- User explicitly requested uGUI for that feature
- Third-party asset requires uGUI and has no UITK path (state why in code or PR)
- Touching legacy uGUI already in scene — prefer migrating to UITK unless user scopes a uGUI-only fix

### Project examples (UITK)

| Feature | Approach |
|---------|----------|
| Exploration / Hub / Combat HUD | `UIDocument` + UXML under `Assets/UI/Screens/` |
| Screen fade | `ScreenFadePresenter` on child `ScreenFade` — UITK overlay, not `Image` |
| Editor tools | UITK `CreateGUI` — see design-docs `unity-editor-ui-toolkit.mdc` |

## Separation of concerns

| Layer | Responsibility |
|-------|----------------|
| **UXML** | Structure, hierarchy, `name` hooks for queries — minimal inline style |
| **USS** | Layout, colors, states, responsive rules |
| **C#** | Behaviour, data binding, events — `MonoBehaviour` / presenter beside `UIDocument` |

Do not embed game logic in UXML. Do not hardcode colours in UXML when a USS class exists.

## BEM naming (UXML + USS classes)

Unity recommends **Block Element Modifier (BEM)** for classes in UXML and USS (same spirit as modern CSS).

Pattern: `block-name__element-name--modifier-name`

| Part | Meaning | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| **Block** | Standalone UI component | `combat-log` |
| **Element** | Part of a block (`__`) | `combat-log__entry` |
| **Modifier** | Variant/state (`--`) | `combat-log--collapsed`, `combat-log__entry--selected` |

Use **kebab-case** within each segment. A BEM name should convey **what** it is, **where** it lives, and **how** it differs from siblings.

- Apply BEM to **`class`** attributes in UXML and selectors in USS — not to C# type names.
- **UXML `name`** (for `Q()`): stable kebab-case id per element — may mirror the element segment (`combat-log__round` → `name="combat-log-round"`) but need not duplicate every modifier class.
- **C# types**: PascalCase — `CombatLogView`
- **Assets**: `CombatHud.uxml`, `CombatHud.uss`, `CombatHudView.cs`

## UXML

- One root screen / panel per primary UXML; reuse **templates** (`<ui:Instance>` / UXML templates) for repeated chunks.
- Assign every element you query in code a **`name`**; avoid querying by fragile class-only paths across refactors.
- Prefer **USS classes** over duplicated inline `style=""` attributes.

```xml
<!-- ✅ GOOD — BEM classes -->
<ui:VisualElement name="combat-log" class="combat-log">
    <ui:Label name="combat-log-round" class="combat-log__round" text="Round 1" />
    <ui:ScrollView class="combat-log__entries combat-log__entries--dense" />
</ui:VisualElement>
```

## USS

- Selectors target **BEM classes** — `.combat-log { }`, `.combat-log__entry { }`, `.combat-log__entry--selected { }`
- **Theme / variables** for shared colours, spacing, fonts — `var(--spacing-md)` on blocks.
- Prefer **modifiers** (`--selected`, `--disabled`) over ad-hoc class names like `.selected-red`.
- Pseudo-states: `:hover`, `:active` on BEM blocks/elements where USS supports them.
- Keep selectors **shallow** — prefer one BEM class per node over long descendant chains.

## C# bindings

- Cache `VisualElement` / control references in `OnEnable` or after `rootVisualElement` is ready — no per-frame `Q` unless necessary.
- Subscribe in `OnEnable` / `RegisterCallback`, **unsubscribe** in `OnDisable` / `UnregisterCallback`.
- Toggle state with **BEM modifier classes** (`element.AddToClassList("combat-log__entry--selected")`) before inline styles.
- Custom controls: inherit `VisualElement`, use `UxmlElement` / `UxmlFactory` + `UxmlTraits` (Unity 6) for designer-friendly UXML.

```csharp
// naming: PascalCase type; query by kebab-case name matching UXML name=""
public class CombatHudView : MonoBehaviour
{
    Label m_roundLabel;

    void OnEnable()
    {
        var root = GetComponent<UIDocument>().rootVisualElement;
        m_roundLabel = root.Q<Label>("combat-log-round");
    }
}
```

## Project layout (recommended)

```
Assets/.../UI/
  Settings/      — GamePanelSettings.asset (shared Panel Settings for UIDocument)
  Screens/       — feature UXML/USS pairs
  Themes/        — default Theme StyleSheet, tokens
  Scripts/       — views / presenters (under Assets/Scripts/UI/)
```

Align namespaces with folders (`GridDungeon.UI.Combat`). Match existing layout if the repo already defines one.

When editing legacy UI, match surrounding UXML/USS patterns unless refactoring that screen.
